    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d536">

                <pb facs="0656" n="548" xml:id="pg548"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wharton to Prince
                            Cantacuzène.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-02-28"><hi rendition="#italic">February 28,
                        1893</hi>.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have had the honor to receive your note
                    of the 20th instant, in reply to Mr. Foster’s of the 16th, concerning the
                    refusal of the Russian consul-general at New York to visé the United States
                    passport of Mrs. Mannie Lerin.</p>
                <p>In view of your statement that the visa in question was refused because Mrs.
                    Lerin “declared herself to be a Jewess,” and in accordance with the instructions
                    of the Imperial Government “interdicting to visé passports of foreign Jews, with
                    the exception of certain cases under which Mrs. Lerin can not be placed,” I
                    limit myself for the present to acknowledging your communication under the
                    reserve necessarily imposed upon the Government by its constitution and laws,
                    and by its just expectation that its certification of the character of American
                    citizenship will be respected.</p>
                <p>Accept, sir, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William E. Wharton</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
